  menuTitle: Pets
  choices:
  - choiceName: Wait for Notice
    carNumberMod: 0
    horseNumberMod: 0
    kidNumberMod: 0
    petNumberMod: 0
    homeHardeningMod: 0
    carSpeedMod: 0
    carLeaveTime: 2
    choiceDetail: 'No city-wide plan requests. Let residents make a decision when
      the evacuation notice goes out. '
    choiceThought: 
    choiceThoughtSprite: {fileID: 0}
    endGameFeedback: "Families with pets took a long time to get out because no one
      was prepared. Next time we need to help them get out quicker, for everyone\u2019s
      safety."
    isLocked: 0
    isNormal: 1
  - choiceName: Plan Ahead
    carNumberMod: 0
    horseNumberMod: 0
    kidNumberMod: 0
    petNumberMod: 0
    homeHardeningMod: 0
    carSpeedMod: 1
    carLeaveTime: 1
    choiceDetail: Before an emergency, prepare carriers, leashes, food, water, and
      medical records. Make sure all animals have collars with identification.
    choiceThought: 
    choiceThoughtSprite: {fileID: 0}
    endGameFeedback: Families with pets planned ahead and were able to get out as
      soon as the evacuation notice came, but maybe they could leave even earlier
      next time.
    isLocked: 1
    isNormal: 0
  - choiceName: 'Evacuate Early  '
    carNumberMod: 0
    horseNumberMod: 0
    kidNumberMod: 0
    petNumberMod: 0
    homeHardeningMod: 0
    carSpeedMod: 2
    carLeaveTime: 0
    choiceDetail: Having pets might make the evacuation stressful, so it is important
      to plan ahead and make sure the pets are in a room where they can be easily
      found for the evacuation.
    choiceThought: 
    choiceThoughtSprite: {fileID: 0}
    endGameFeedback: "\u201CGood call for families with pets to evacuate early, they
      all got out of the path of the fire before an evacuation notice came!"
    isLocked: 1
    isNormal: 0
  longerTitle: Pets
  descriptions:
  - Having pets can increase the time needed to evacuate safely. It is very important
    to make plans in advance and consider multiple scenarios and consequences of
    choices.
  - ' Be sure you have a carrier or leash readily available and that the pets are
    in an easily accessible space on red flag days (ie. keep them inside and in a
    room where you can find/catch them easily). It may be a good idea to evacuate
    early and have a plan to stay in a pet-friendly shelter or with friends. '
